.TH NM 1
.SH NAME
nm \- print the symbol table
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nm
[\ \-gnopru\ ] [\ file\ ...\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nm
prints the symbol table, the names of the functions, variables and
labels and what they stand for, of each
.IR file :
an executable program, an object file, or a library of object files, in
which case the symbols of each member are printed under its name. With
no
.IR file ,
nm prints the symbols of a.out.
.PP
Each symbol is printed on a line with its value in hexadecimal, a
letter for its type, and its name:
.TP 6
.B T
text: a function or a label in the code; the value is its address
.TP
.B D
data: an initialized variable
.TP
.B B
bss: an uninitialized variable, set to zeros when the program starts
.TP
.B A
absolute: a value that is not an address
.TP
.B C
common: in an object file, an uninitialized external variable that
several files may declare; the value is its size in bytes
.TP
.B U
undefined: used in this file, and defined in another one; no value is
printed
.PP
A lower case letter means the symbol is local to its file (for
instance, a C function or variable declared static). The names of C
functions and external variables begin with an underscore:
.B main
is
.BR _main .
The symbols are sorted by name.
.PP
The options are:
.TP 6
.B \-g
Print only external (global) symbols.
.TP
.B \-u
Print only undefined symbols.
.TP
.B \-n
Sort by value instead of by name.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the order of the sort.
.TP
.B \-p
Do not sort: print the symbols in the order of the symbol table.
.TP
.B \-o
Put the file's name, and the library member's, at the front of every
line, instead of in a heading before each file's symbols: the form to
search with grep.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
	nm \-g prog		the program's external symbols
	nm \-u prog.o		what an object file needs from others
	nm \-o /usr/lib/cc/libc.a | grep printf
				which members of the C library
				define or use printf
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"no symbols" for a program linked with
.B "cc \-s"
or stripped. "not an executable, object file or archive" for any other kind of file. The
exit status is 1 if a file could not be read or had no symbols, and 0
otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
cc(1), ld86(1), size(1), strip(1), ar86(1), adb(1)
.SH HISTORY
An
.B nm
command appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX.
.SH AUTHORS
This nm was written for the xenix86-devsys Development System.
